/**
 * Grade 8 informational articles: 50 of them, five for each of RI.8.1 to
 * RI.8.10, alternating science and social studies.
 *
 * Held to the bands in scripts/reading-article-plan.json: seven paragraphs,
 * a higher version at LL 1090-1190 and a lower one at LL 870-970 with the
 * same lettered paragraphs, five questions of the five named kinds.
 *
 * What grade 8 adds to grade 7. RI.8.1 does not ask for evidence, it asks
 * for the evidence that supports a claim MOST STRONGLY, which means a
 * reader has to rank what they found. RI.8.8 asks them to recognise
 * evidence that is irrelevant rather than merely weak. RI.8.9 asks them to
 * say whether two texts disagree about a fact or about what the fact
 * means, which is the distinction most arguments hide in.
 *
 * So these articles are chosen to contain a ranking, a category boundary
 * or a disagreement whose nature can be named. Several of them turn on a
 * prediction made before the evidence existed, because a prediction is the
 * cleanest example there is of one piece of evidence outweighing another.
 *
 *   bun run scripts/articles/g8.src.mjs && bun run scripts/merge-articles.mjs
 *   bun run scripts/check-articles.mjs
 */
