      "Three changes built the category. Compulsory schooling held young people in institutions for longer, child labour laws removed them from workplaces, and both together produced a large group of people who were neither children nor workers.",
      "A psychologist named G. Stanley Hall had argued in 1904 that adolescence was a distinct developmental stage with its own characteristics. The claim gave the emerging group a scientific description to be understood through.",
      "Then came the market. Postwar American firms noticed a population with some money, time and no dependants, and products, magazines and music were made specifically for them, which made the category visible to itself.",
      "Once a category exists it generates comparisons. Laws now distinguish by age in dozens of places, from driving to contracts, and each of those lines has to be drawn somewhere even though development does not happen on a birthday.",
      "The useful question is not whether teenagers are real. It is which parts of the category describe something about human development and which parts describe a particular society's schooling, labour laws and shops.",
