      "The convention is a single paragraph built from repeated clauses: in this Act, vehicle means, employee means, premises means. The repetition is deliberate, because a definition that varies in form invites argument about whether the variation meant something.",
      "Each clause is doing one of two opposite jobs. Some widen an ordinary word so it covers more than usual, and some narrow it so a familiar word excludes cases a reader would assume were included.",
      "The widening clauses are the ones to watch. A definition saying vehicle includes any device for carrying a person by land is reaching for things nobody would call a vehicle in conversation, and that reach is the law's actual scope.",
      "There is usually a sentence near the end doing something different again. It says that where a word is not defined here it carries its ordinary meaning, which quietly puts a dictionary into the statute.",
      "The order within the paragraph matters too. A term used inside another definition has to be defined before or alongside it, so the sequence often reveals which concept the drafters treated as basic.",
      "Reading this section first changes the rest. A sentence later in the law that looked clear often turns out to be using at least one word in a sense that was fixed in a paragraph the reader skipped.",
