      "A scientific journal does not only print papers. It prints comments on papers, and replies to those comments, and the whole exchange is published under the names of the people having it, in the same volume as the work being argued about.",
      "The sequence is fixed. Someone publishes a result, another researcher writes a comment arguing it is wrong, the original authors reply, and all three appear in the record where anyone can read them together.",
      "What a comment does is narrower than disagreement. It identifies a specific step: an analysis that should have excluded certain data, a control that was missing, an alternative explanation the paper did not rule out.",
      "The reply is the interesting document, because a reply has only a few honest moves available. The authors can show the step was handled and say where, they can accept the point and state what survives it, or they can present new analysis.",
      "What a reply cannot do is ignore the objection and restate the original claim more firmly. Readers can see both texts at once, and a reply that does not engage is visible as such immediately.",
      "The exchange also fixes a date. After a published comment, the objection is on the record, so later work that repeats the original claim without addressing it is making a choice rather than an oversight.",
      "This is why a field's disagreements are worth reading rather than avoiding. A paper on its own shows what its authors concluded; a paper with its comment and reply shows what the authors could defend when someone pushed.",
