      "The order protects against a specific failure. An author who reads the studies first and then writes the inclusion rules can produce any answer they want, simply by setting a boundary that excludes the inconvenient ones.",
      "The search is then meant to be exhaustive rather than convenient. Reviewers search several databases, look for unpublished work, and write down how many studies they found and how many they excluded at each stage, with reasons.",
      "Each included study is assessed for risk of bias. A trial where participants knew which group they were in is not weighted the same as one where nobody did, and the assessment is reported study by study rather than averaged away.",
      "Only then are results combined, and sometimes they should not be. If the studies measured different things in different populations, a single pooled number hides the disagreement instead of resolving it, and a careful review says so.",
