      "The distinction is about reach. A mislabelled axis or a wrong sample size in a table is corrected; an error that undermines the result, or evidence that the data were not what they were said to be, produces a retraction.",
      "Both are published rather than quietly applied. The original paper stays in the record with a notice attached, because papers that have already been cited cannot be removed from the work that cited them.",
      "Authors do not always initiate these. Some corrections follow an outside reader finding the problem, and some retractions are issued by a journal over the authors' objection, and the notice usually says which happened.",
      "That makes the wording of a notice worth reading. A notice saying the authors identified an error and reported it describes a different event from one saying the journal was unable to obtain the original data.",
      "The incentives run against admitting anything. A correction is a public statement that a published result was wrong, and the people best placed to find an error are the authors, who have the least to gain from finding it.",
      "Which is why the researchers who correct their own work are worth noticing. The number of corrections a field publishes is not a measure of how often it is wrong; it is a measure of how often it says so.",
