      "The response is active, not passive. Digital preservation means copying to new media and migrating to current formats on a schedule. That is a continuing cost, not a one-off purchase.",
      "That changes what an archive is. A paper archive survives neglect and is destroyed by fire or flood. A digital archive survives fire easily and is destroyed by nobody paying the bill.",
      "The failures are differently visible too. A damaged page announces itself. A corrupted file may open and display what looks like the document, with a section silently missing.",
      "So serious archives use both. The original is the thing itself and degrades slowly. The scan can be read, searched and copied. Neither replaces the other.",
